//!
//! # Web
//!
//! A minimally opinionated web framework in the spirit of Express, built on
//! `std::http`. Middleware and routes take the request, the response and the
//! next function, and return `Result<void, Web::Error>`; an error that
//! reaches the router becomes a response with its status.
//!
//! ## Exports
//!
//! | Export              | Description                                                         |
//! |---------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------|
//! | `Web::Router`       | `new()`, `use`, `get`, `post`, `put`, `patch`, `delete`, `any`, `route`, `dispatch`; implements `Http::Handler` |
//! | `Web::App`          | Trait: `build(&self): Web::Router`, called once per server thread   |
//! | `Web::listen`       | Parses `"127.0.0.1:8080"` and serves an app with the default configuration |
//! | `Web::listenOn`     | Serves an app from a host and a port                                |
//! | `Web::listenWith`   | Serves an app from an address and a `Web::Config`                   |
//! | `Web::parseAddress` | Parses `"<host>:<port>"`; `":8080"` and `"*:8080"` are every interface |
//! | `Web::Config`       | `threads`; `new()`, `toServerConfig()`                              |
//! | `Web::ListenError`  | A failed start; `message()` renders what was expected or the bind failure |
//! | `Web::serve`        | The layer below: binds a `SocketAddress` and serves an app on `ServerConfig.threads` threads |
//! | `Web::serveListener` | The layer below: serves an app on a listener already bound         |
//! | `Web::Middleware`   | `(&mut Request, &mut Response, Next) -> Result<void, Error>`        |
//! | `Web::Next`         | `(&mut Request, &mut Response) -> Result<void, Error>`              |
//! | `Web::Request`      | `http`; `create(&http)`, `method()`, `target()`, `path()`, `header(name)`, `body()`, `bodyText()`, `param(name)`, `query(name)` |
//! | `Web::Response`     | `status`, `headers`, `body`; `new()`, `setStatus`, `header`, `setHeader`, `addHeader`, `write`, `writeStr`, `text`, `json`, `finish` |
//! | `Web::Error`        | `status`, `message`; `new(message)` (500), `withStatus(status, message)` |
//! | `Web::Param`        | One route parameter: `name` and decoded `value`                     |
//!
//! ## Starting a server
//!
//! `Web::listen` parses an address string, binds it and serves the app:
//! `"127.0.0.1:8080"` binds that interface, and `":8080"` or `"*:8080"`
//! binds every interface, `0.0.0.0`. `Web::listenOn` takes a host and a
//! port, `Web::listenWith` a `Web::Config` (the thread count), and
//! `Web::parseAddress` parses without serving. A malformed address is a
//! `Web::ListenError` whose message names what was expected and what was
//! given; names are not resolved. `Web::serve` and `Web::serveListener`
//! are the layer below: they take the `std::net::SocketAddress` and the
//! `std::http::ServerConfig` directly.
//!
//! ## Middleware and routes
//!
//! Both have the `Web::Middleware` shape. A middleware added with `use`
//! runs for every request, in the order it was added, before the routes.
//! Calling `next` runs the rest of the chain and returns once it finished,
//! so code after `next` runs on the way back out (the onion model), and an
//! error from `next` propagates with `!` like any other. `next` takes the
//! request and the response back as arguments: a closure holding them would
//! keep them borrowed for as long as it lives.
//!
//! A middleware that does not call `next` ends the chain; returning an
//! error does too. A route that calls `next` passes the request to the next
//! route that matches it; when there is none, the answer is 404.
//!
//! ## Routing
//!
//! A route matches by method and path. `get` also answers `HEAD` (the
//! server sends the head without the body); `any` answers every method;
//! `route` takes any method name as written on the request line.
//!
//! The path is compared segment by segment, before any `?` and without
//! decoding. A segment `:name` matches any non-empty segment and makes its
//! percent-decoded value available as `request.param("name")`; any other
//! segment matches only itself. Every segment counts, so a trailing slash
//! is significant: `/users/:id` matches `/users/7` but not `/users/7/`, and
//! `/users/` does not match `/users`. A parameter that is not valid
//! percent-encoded UTF-8 is answered with 400.
//!
//! When no route matches, the answer is 404. When some route matches the
//! path but none its method, the answer is 405 with an `Allow` header
//! listing the methods that would match. Both run through the middleware
//! as errors, so a logger sees them.
//!
//! `request.query("name")` decodes the first query parameter with that
//! name: `+` is a space, `%XX` an escaped byte, and a parameter written
//! without `=` has an empty value. A value that is not valid
//! percent-encoded UTF-8 is `NONE`.
//!
//! ## Errors
//!
//! `Web::Error` carries a status (500 by default) and a message. The router
//! answers an error with that status, `Content-Type: text/plain;
//! charset=utf-8` and the message as the body, keeping header fields that
//! were already set. Client input never makes the router panic.
//!
//! ## Threads
//!
//! Function values are neither `Send` nor `Sync`, so a router cannot be
//! shared between threads. An application is a record implementing
//! `Web::App`; `Web::serve` shares it among `ServerConfig.threads` threads,
//! each of which calls `build` once and serves its connections with its own
//! router through `Http::acceptConnections`. State shared by every thread
//! lives in the app record and has to be `Send` and `Sync`, behind an `Arc`,
//! a `Mutex` or an atomic.
//!
//! A router is also an `Http::Handler`, so `Http::serveConnection` serves
//! one connection with it, which is how a test drives it.
//!
//! ## Example
//!
//! ```ignis
//! import Io from "std::io";
//! import Web from "std::web";
//!
//! function logger(
//!   request: &mut Web::Request,
//!   response: &mut Web::Response,
//!   next: Web::Next
//! ): Result<void, Web::Error> {
//!   let result: Result<void, Web::Error> = next(request, response);
//!   Io::println(`${request.method().toString()} ${request.path()} ${response.status}`);
//!   return result;
//! }
//!
//! function showUser(
//!   request: &mut Web::Request,
//!   response: &mut Web::Response,
//!   next: Web::Next
//! ): Result<void, Web::Error> {
//!   let Option::SOME(id) = request.param("id") else {
//!     return Result::ERROR(Web::Error::withStatus(400, "missing id"));
//!   };
//!
//!   let text: String = String::create(id);
//!
//!   if (text.equals("0")) {
//!     return Result::ERROR(Web::Error::withStatus(404, "no such user"));
//!   }
//!
//!   response.text(`user ${id}`);
//!   return Result::OK(());
//! }
//!
//! @implements(Web::App)
//! record Users {
//!   public greeting: str;
//!
//!   build(&self): Web::Router {
//!     let greeting: str = self.greeting;
//!     let mut router: Web::Router = Web::Router::new();
//!     router.use(logger);
//!     router.get(
//!       "/",
//!       (request: &mut Web::Request, response: &mut Web::Response, next: Web::Next): Result<void, Web::Error> -> {
//!         response.text(greeting);
//!         return Result::OK(());
//!       },
//!     );
//!     router.get("/users/:id", showUser);
//!     return router;
//!   }
//! }
//!
//! function main(): i32 {
//!   let app: Users = Users { greeting: "hello" };
//!
//!   return Web::listen<Users>("127.0.0.1:8080", app).isOk() ? 0 : 1;
//! }
//! ```

import _ from "std::web::listen";
import _ from "std::web::request";
import _ from "std::web::router";

export Web from "./router";
